WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.660
He was the father of Fascism, the iron fisted
dictator who ruled Italy for more than two

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:06.660
decades.

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:10.889
During that time, he plunged his country to
disaster, forming an alliance with Adolf Hitler

00:00:10.889 --> 00:00:14.540
and bringing the wrath of the world – and
his own people – upon him.

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:18.529
In the end, his own citizens expressed their
own ruthless verdict on the man who called

00:00:18.529 --> 00:00:19.780
himself Il Duce.

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:28.300
In this weeks, Biographics we track the life
and death of Benito Mussolini.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:31.679
Beginnings

00:00:31.679 --> 00:00:42.960
Benito Amilcare Andrea Mussolini was born
on the 29th of July, 1883 in a small village

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:47.100
near the northern Italian town of Predappio
in the province of Forli.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:51.760
His father, Allesandro, was a blacksmith and
an atheist who was heavily involved in Socialist

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:56.060
politics, while his mother, Rosa, was a devout
Roman Catholic schoolteacher.

00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:59.920
With his parents’ divergent beliefs, the
young Mussolini was raised to put his faith

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:02.190
in both Karl Marx and the Pope.

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:05.679
His mother took him to mass several times
a week, while his father dragged him to the

00:01:05.679 --> 00:01:08.320
pub to learn from his Socialist friends.

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:11.950
From the start, Benito proved to be an aggressive,
rebellious child.

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:13.119
In later life reflected . . .

00:01:13.119 --> 00:01:14.540
I was not a good boy.

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:16.789
I was, I believe, unruly.

00:01:16.789 --> 00:01:20.890
By the time he was eight, Benito was spending
long hours helping his father in the furnace.

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:26.460
Here he would listen to Allseandro’s socialist
ideas and these were to mould his future philosophy.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:28.500
But the boy’s heart was with his mother.

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:33.170
He feared displeasing her, yet, this did not
curb his wayward tendencies.

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:37.200
By the time he was nine, Rosa had become so
exasperated with Benito that she sent him

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:42.999
to a school run by Roman Catholic monks in
order to instil some much-needed discipline.

00:01:42.999 --> 00:01:48.560
But the defiant Benito chafed at the strict
discipline and his behaviour became even worse.

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:53.329
He got into a number of fights and was eventually
expelled when he took on one of the monks.

00:01:53.329 --> 00:01:57.750
Rosa now sent her wayward son to a less strict
school closer to home.

00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:02.150
This did nothing to improve his behaviour
and before long he was again expelled, this

00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:05.799
time for threatening another student with
a knife.

00:02:05.799 --> 00:02:10.450
His exasperated mother managed to find a third
school, where he managed to see out his schooling.

00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:14.380
At the age of seventeen, Benito completed
his ten years of compulsory schooling.

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:19.640
His main accomplishments over that period
were the ability to play the trombone and

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:20.640
to speak in public.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:25.030
However, despite his poor academic record,
in 1901, he qualified as a school teacher.

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:28.000
But, he soon discovered that teaching did
not suit him.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:33.860
He was far more interested in the two passions
that he had inherited from his father – socialism

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:35.190
and womanizing.

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:39.550
The eighteen-year old Benito was a strong,
handsome young man with a forceful personality

00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:41.010
and a certain charisma.

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:44.910
He easily drew the attention of young women,
often the wives of other men.

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:49.460
This often resulted in fist fights with outraged
husbands.

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:51.410
Rabble Rouser

00:02:51.410 --> 00:02:57.470
After less than a year, Mussolini had lost
his teaching job after getting into a violent

00:02:57.470 --> 00:02:58.470
argument.

00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:01.750
With no money and very little prospects, he
moved to Switzerland.

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:04.530
He worked in a succession of manual jobs to
support himself.

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:08.470
Within a short time, he had attached himself
to a group of Marxists.

00:03:08.470 --> 00:03:13.150
He also joined a trade union and began attending
rival political rallies, where he would heckle

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:16.220
the speaker and start fights with those in
the crowd.

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:20.640
As a result of these political agitations,
Benito was arrested and deported back to Italy

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:21.760
several times.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:27.460
But each time he returned, more determined
than ever to keep stirring the political pot.

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:30.120
Mussolini struggled to make ends meet in Switzerland.

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:34.230
On one occasion he accosted two elderly women
on the street and stole food from them.

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:39.120
In his 1928 autobiography, he related that
if the women had struggled he would have strangled

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:40.120
them.

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:42.980
In 1904, he returned to Italy and joined the
Italian army.

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:45.790
He served out the compulsory eighteen months.

00:03:45.790 --> 00:03:49.850
Ten months into his military service, Mussolini’s
beloved mother, Rosa, died.

00:03:49.850 --> 00:03:54.030
A distraught Benito later called this the
greatest sorrow of his life.

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:56.510
He was discharged from the army in September,
1906.

00:03:56.510 --> 00:04:01.150
He had clearly not learned discipline while
in his country’s service as his life now

00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:05.790
revolved around drinking, womanizing and fighting.

00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:09.610
Fervent Socialist

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:14.420
He managed to gain employment as a teacher
again with a succession of temporary contracts.

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:18.709
During this period prior to the First World
War, Mussolini’s moral character deteriorated

00:04:18.709 --> 00:04:20.650
as his socialist ardor increased.

00:04:20.650 --> 00:04:24.530
He wrote for and edited several socialist
newspapers, railing against the government,

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:28.129
the democratic system, the middle classes
and the church.

00:04:28.129 --> 00:04:32.870
In one pamphlet, entitled God Does Not Exist,
he wrote that priests were ‘black microbes,

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:37.210
who are as fatal to mankind as tuberculosis
germs.’

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:41.879
When Italy engaged in a war against Libya
in 1911, Mussolini led the internal criticism

00:04:41.879 --> 00:04:44.940
against the government’s actions through
his newspaper writings.

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:48.610
His outspoken opposition led to his arrest
and a five-month imprisonment.

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:53.319
Yet his fist shaking public opposition to
the government had also garnered the attention

00:04:53.319 --> 00:04:57.280
of the country’s top Socialist leaders who
looked on admiringly.

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:02.180
The young Benito Mussolini was viewed by them
as a rising star of the left.

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:06.520
In December,1912, shortly after his release
from prison, Benito was appointed editor of

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:10.020
the country’s national socialist newspaper,
Avanti.

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:14.569
It was a natural fit and within six months,
his firebrand style of journalism had increased

00:05:14.569 --> 00:05:16.830
the papers readership five-fold.

00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:21.300
He now had a national forum with which to
air his views.

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:25.960
The First World War

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:30.889
When war broke out in 1914, Mussolini held
firm non-interventionist views.

00:05:30.889 --> 00:05:36.099
He saw war as anti-socialist as it pitted
the working classes of one nation against

00:05:36.099 --> 00:05:37.320
those of another.

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:39.289
Within months, though, he had changed his
view.

00:05:39.289 --> 00:05:41.719
He now saw war as an opportunity to foment
revolution.

00:05:41.719 --> 00:05:46.190
He now used his voice through the pages of
Avanti to call on the young men of Italy to

00:05:46.190 --> 00:05:47.300
join the army.

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:51.410
Mussolini’s about face on the war situation
put him at odds with more moderate socialist

00:05:51.410 --> 00:05:57.340
leaders and resulted in his expulsion from
the party and loss of editorship of Avanti.

00:05:57.340 --> 00:06:02.569
Defiantly he started his own newspaper, ‘Il
Populo Italia’ or ‘The People of Italy.’

00:06:02.569 --> 00:06:07.270
This paper ran continuously from November
1914 until Mussolini’s removal from power

00:06:07.270 --> 00:06:08.999
twenty-nine years later.

00:06:08.999 --> 00:06:12.099
In his first editorial he proclaimed the famous
line . . .

00:06:12.099 --> 00:06:15.449
Blood alone moves the wheels of history.

00:06:15.449 --> 00:06:20.259
Around this same time, Mussolini founded his
first political party – the Fascii of Revolutionary

00:06:20.259 --> 00:06:21.259
Action.

00:06:21.259 --> 00:06:23.539
This was soon simply known as the Fascist
Party.

00:06:23.539 --> 00:06:27.759
The main Allied Powers, Britain and France,
supported Mussolini, seeing him as a key to

00:06:27.759 --> 00:06:30.099
getting Italy more involved in the war effort.

00:06:30.099 --> 00:06:33.219
They provided much needed financial support
to the new party.

00:06:33.219 --> 00:06:37.909
The British Secret Service even paid Mussolini
a wage of one hundred pounds per week.

00:06:37.909 --> 00:06:42.509
In July 1915, Italy signed the Treaty of London,
committing itself to fighting alongside the

00:06:42.509 --> 00:06:44.379
Allies against Germany.

00:06:44.379 --> 00:06:47.220
But Italy’s war soon became a fiasco.

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:52.719
It was plagued by poorly trained and inexperienced
officers and unwilling, belligerent conscripts.

00:06:52.719 --> 00:06:57.159
They became bogged down in a battle of attrition
against Austria-Hungary.

00:06:57.159 --> 00:07:02.819
By the time it was all over more than 650,000
Italians were dead, half a million were missing

00:07:02.819 --> 00:07:05.099
and nearly a million were wounded.

00:07:05.099 --> 00:07:08.360
Mussolini himself re-joined the army in September,
1915.

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:12.699
He attained the rank of corporal, winning
acclaim for his bravery and devotion to his

00:07:12.699 --> 00:07:13.699
men.

00:07:13.699 --> 00:07:18.680
On February 22, 1917, he was in a trench when
one of his fellow soldier’s grenades exploded.

00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:23.159
He was badly wounded, requiring numerous operations
over the course of the next month and then

00:07:23.159 --> 00:07:24.960
being sent home to recuperate.

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:30.069
Mussolini’s message now morphed from Socialism
and the plight of the working man to patriotism

00:07:30.069 --> 00:07:32.039
and the cause of ultra-nationalism.

00:07:32.039 --> 00:07:38.009
Rise of the Fascists

00:07:38.009 --> 00:07:42.110
During the post war talks in Paris, Italy
was treated dismissively by the major powers.

00:07:42.110 --> 00:07:46.729
The territories that she had been promised
during the 1915 treaty of London were not

00:07:46.729 --> 00:07:47.729
handed over.

00:07:47.729 --> 00:07:52.479
The Italian Prime Minister, Vittorio Orlando,
walked out of the conference in disgust.

00:07:52.479 --> 00:07:57.199
Orlando bore the brunt of public discontent
and was soon removed from office.

00:07:57.199 --> 00:08:01.619
In the following elections, Mussolini’s
Fascist Party failed to win a single seat.

00:08:01.619 --> 00:08:05.389
His political rivals made fun of this, staging
a mock funeral for the party.

00:08:05.389 --> 00:08:09.879
Chief among his adversaries were the Socialists,
who had won a third of the seats in parliament

00:08:09.879 --> 00:08:12.439
and who Mussolini was now firmly opposed to.

00:08:12.439 --> 00:08:17.339
Over the next three years, the political situation
in Italy was etremely unstable, with four

00:08:17.339 --> 00:08:22.259
prime ministers and coalition governments,
none of which was able to yield any real power.

00:08:22.259 --> 00:08:26.939
The country was in a state of near anarchy,
with rampant inflation and unemployment driving

00:08:26.939 --> 00:08:28.619
people to despair.

00:08:28.619 --> 00:08:30.110
Mussolini’s Fascist’s.

00:08:30.110 --> 00:08:34.180
dressed in their intimidating black shirts,
roamed through the streets, seeking out and

00:08:34.180 --> 00:08:38.370
beating up Socialists, Communist or anyone
one else they didn’t like the look of.

00:08:38.370 --> 00:08:42.780
The police, who had sympathy for the Fascists,
usually stood by and let Mussolini’s men

00:08:42.780 --> 00:08:44.139
get on with it.

00:08:44.139 --> 00:08:47.389
On May 15, 1921 national elections were held.

00:08:47.389 --> 00:08:51.680
This time, the Fascists joined a coalition
of right-wing parties, the National Block,

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:57.149
and won 35 seats out of the total of 535 in
the House of Parliament.

00:08:57.149 --> 00:09:00.029
Mussolini himself gained a seat in the chamber
of deputies.

00:09:00.029 --> 00:09:03.389
He was now an official member of the Italian
government.

00:09:03.389 --> 00:09:08.470
Realizing that he now needed to broaden his
base, Mussolini did an about face on a couple

00:09:08.470 --> 00:09:09.800
of his underpinning principals.

00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:13.389
He became both pro-monarchy and pro-church.

00:09:13.389 --> 00:09:17.050
But his hatred of socialism remained.

00:09:17.050 --> 00:09:21.440
Mussolini renamed the party, now calling it
the National Fascist Party.

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:25.720
His reputation began to grow and he became
known as a man with the ability to rule with

00:09:25.720 --> 00:09:30.860
a firm hand and restore order amid the chaos
that Italy had fallen into.

00:09:30.860 --> 00:09:34.430
Public opinion was beginning to turn in his
favor.

00:09:34.430 --> 00:09:38.690
That popularity was bolstered when Mussolini’
Black Shirts broke up a Socialist backed strike

00:09:38.690 --> 00:09:40.139
in October, 1922.

00:09:40.139 --> 00:09:44.470
At a rally shortly thereafter, Mussolini declared
in a speech that either the government would

00:09:44.470 --> 00:09:47.940
hand over power to him or he would seize it
for himself.

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:49.800
This was no idle bluster.

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:54.860
With no sign of capitulation by the government,
Mussolini decided to stage a coup by marching

00:09:54.860 --> 00:09:55.860
on Rome.

00:09:55.860 --> 00:10:00.920
His forces were no match for the Italian army
but still the Prime Minister, Luigi Facta,

00:10:00.920 --> 00:10:05.019
offered Mussolini a position in his government
in order to avoid conflict.

00:10:05.019 --> 00:10:09.290
The offer was roundly rejected and the fascists
marched on the capital.

00:10:09.290 --> 00:10:13.000
The panicked Prime Minister urged the King
to allow him to use the army to quash the

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:14.000
rebellion.

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:18.420
The king agreed but quickly changed his mind,
fearing civil war.

00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:21.990
This enraged the Prime Minister who resigned
his office.

00:10:21.990 --> 00:10:26.060
The twenty thousand Fascists marching on the
capital stopped twenty miles north of the

00:10:26.060 --> 00:10:27.060
city.

00:10:27.060 --> 00:10:28.790
There, half of them left off and returned
home.

00:10:28.790 --> 00:10:32.520
The rest carried on, with Mussolini himself
joining them at various points to have his

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:34.759
photo taken at the front of the line.

00:10:34.759 --> 00:10:38.920
But with the rain pouring down he was content
to leave the heavy marching to others and

00:10:38.920 --> 00:10:40.360
took an express train into Rome.

00:10:40.360 --> 00:10:45.040
The king, who secretly admired Mussolini,
now tried to placate him by offering him a

00:10:45.040 --> 00:10:46.120
governmental role.

00:10:46.120 --> 00:10:49.639
Mussolini would consider nothing but the prime
ministership.

00:10:49.639 --> 00:10:52.440
Eventually, the king offered this position
to him.

00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:57.870
At age 39, Mussolini had become Prime Minister,
not through violent revolution, but through

00:10:57.870 --> 00:11:00.920
threat, bluster and unequivocal demand.

00:11:00.920 --> 00:11:05.389
In the wake of his victory, ecstatic Fascist
roamed the streets in search of down heartened

00:11:05.389 --> 00:11:08.149
Socialists that they could terrorize.

00:11:08.149 --> 00:11:12.819
Attaining Absolute Power

00:11:12.819 --> 00:11:15.680
Mussolini was now both Prime Minister and
Foreign Minister.

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:19.060
But his government was very much a minority
in the House of Parliament.

00:11:19.060 --> 00:11:22.980
This forced him to work with the confines
of democracy, which left him at the mercy

00:11:22.980 --> 00:11:24.730
of the majority coalition.

00:11:24.730 --> 00:11:29.130
This was not a situation that the power-hungry
autocrat would tolerate for long.

00:11:29.130 --> 00:11:33.870
Within eight weeks of assuming office, Mussolini
formed the Fascist Grand Council.

00:11:33.870 --> 00:11:38.899
The Grand Council acted as a conduit between
the Fascists and the Chamber of Deputies.

00:11:38.899 --> 00:11:42.990
Filled with his hand picked appointees, it
was, in effect, a mouthpiece for Mussolini

00:11:42.990 --> 00:11:46.839
which effectively lessened the power of Parliament
to act.

00:11:46.839 --> 00:11:51.860
In 1923 the Grand Council passed a law that
rigged the electoral system to ensure that

00:11:51.860 --> 00:11:55.829
the Fascists gained a sizable majority in
the next election.

00:11:55.829 --> 00:12:02.340
When the election was held the following April,
the Fascist won 374 of the 535 seats.

00:12:02.340 --> 00:12:08.389
The new law helped to gain this 70% majority,
but so too did the vigilant presence of Mussolini’s

00:12:08.389 --> 00:12:12.209
Black Shirts, who coerced the public to vote
for the Fascists.

00:12:12.209 --> 00:12:16.360
In addition, opposition party meetings were
broken up and their candidates beaten.

00:12:16.360 --> 00:12:21.120
In the wake of this overwhelming electoral
victory, socialist leader, Giacomo Matteoti,

00:12:21.120 --> 00:12:23.959
openly denounced the Fascists and their leader.

00:12:23.959 --> 00:12:26.530
Eleven days later, Matteoti was assassinated.

00:12:26.530 --> 00:12:31.279
This led to nationwide protest against Mussolini
and his party of thugs, with many people calling

00:12:31.279 --> 00:12:33.240
on the king to depose the Prime Minister.

00:12:33.240 --> 00:12:38.129
For his part, Mussolini pleaded innocence
and acted shocked and revolted by the murder.

00:12:38.129 --> 00:12:40.800
He did his best to distance himself from the
crime.

00:12:40.800 --> 00:12:45.470
Still, Politicians from all parties responded
by withdrawing from Parliament in an effort

00:12:45.470 --> 00:12:47.590
to force Mussolini’s ejection from office.

00:12:47.590 --> 00:12:49.889
But the king refused to get rid of Mussolini.

00:12:49.889 --> 00:12:54.860
In a vote of confidence on January 3rd,1925,
few of the Prime Minister’s opponents showed

00:12:54.860 --> 00:12:55.860
up.

00:12:55.860 --> 00:12:59.529
Ever the opportunist, he now took the opportunity
to cement his absolute authority.

00:12:59.529 --> 00:13:05.160
He declared that only the Fascists could provide
stability in the country and it would be achieved

00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:08.519
through dictatorship.

00:13:08.519 --> 00:13:11.699
Totalitarianism

00:13:11.699 --> 00:13:16.379
As Mussolini’s propaganda machine infiltrated
every area of Italy, a cult of personality

00:13:16.379 --> 00:13:17.610
developed around him.

00:13:17.610 --> 00:13:21.820
People considered him to be a genius man of
action, but one with the common touch.

00:13:21.820 --> 00:13:25.509
When things went well, it was all thanks to
Il Duce (the leader) However, when things

00:13:25.509 --> 00:13:27.750
went wrong it was never his fault.

00:13:27.750 --> 00:13:32.600
He was a mesmerizing speaker, who used facial
expressions and gestures to capture and enthral

00:13:32.600 --> 00:13:33.600
his audience.

00:13:33.600 --> 00:13:38.529
He stood only 5’6” inches tall and liked
to dress in striped trousers and shirts with

00:13:38.529 --> 00:13:39.920
butterfly collars.

00:13:39.920 --> 00:13:43.970
He was not a well man physically, suffering
from ulcers and stomach upset.

00:13:43.970 --> 00:13:46.970
His health problems forced him to give up
smoking and drinking.

00:13:46.970 --> 00:13:50.220
Beginning to lose his hair in his late twenties,
he took to shaving his head.

00:13:50.220 --> 00:13:53.759
He was also short sighted but too vain to
wear glasses.

00:13:53.759 --> 00:13:57.910
Mussolini spent the next two years dismantling
democracy in Italy and establishing his own

00:13:57.910 --> 00:13:59.829
totalitarian regime.

00:13:59.829 --> 00:14:03.459
During that time, he instilled three key messages
into the public consciousness . . .

00:14:03.459 --> 00:14:06.790
Believe, Obey, Fight

00:14:06.790 --> 00:14:09.180
This message gained widespread approval.

00:14:09.180 --> 00:14:12.189
Even many former Socialists now turned to
Fascism.

00:14:12.189 --> 00:14:17.189
The firm hand of the state was seen as the
only solution to the country’s woes.

00:14:17.189 --> 00:14:21.509
All forms of criticism of the state were banned
and opposition parties disbanded.

00:14:21.509 --> 00:14:25.260
Mussolini himself was answerable to only the
king.

00:14:25.260 --> 00:14:29.420
Spies were everywhere, eager to report to
the authorities anyone who spoke or acted

00:14:29.420 --> 00:14:30.800
against the state.

00:14:30.800 --> 00:14:34.970
During the 1920’s, the Fascists had made
modest improvements in the Italian economy.

00:14:34.970 --> 00:14:39.040
However, all of this was overturned with the
global depression that followed the Wall Street

00:14:39.040 --> 00:14:41.120
Crash in October, 1929.

00:14:41.120 --> 00:14:44.589
Once again, the country was in a state of
economic chaos.

00:14:44.589 --> 00:14:47.850
Mussolini responded by embarking on foreign
invasions.

00:14:47.850 --> 00:14:52.370
During the 1930’s he sent his armies into
Libya, Ethiopia and Spain.

00:14:52.370 --> 00:14:57.709
Italy’s invasion of Ethiopia began in 1935
and resulted in a devastating occupation that

00:14:57.709 --> 00:15:02.399
would continue until 1941, when the British
army forced the Italians out.

00:15:02.399 --> 00:15:07.579
During that time it has been estimate that
8% of Ethiopia’s population was put to death.

00:15:07.579 --> 00:15:11.759
Mussolini ordered that the male populations
of entire towns be wiped out.

00:15:11.759 --> 00:15:15.579
Ethiopia’s leader, Haile Selassie, was forced
to flee to England from where he petitioned

00:15:15.579 --> 00:15:16.579
the United Nations.

00:15:16.579 --> 00:15:17.579
They eventually imposed sanctions on Italy.

00:15:17.579 --> 00:15:22.490
This infuriated Mussolini and sent him into
the arms of aneighboring dictator by the name

00:15:22.490 --> 00:15:24.590
of Adolf Hitler.

00:15:24.590 --> 00:15:29.360
Pact of Steel

00:15:29.360 --> 00:15:35.470
On May 22, 1939, Hitler and Mussolini signed
a Pact of Steel, by which they promised to

00:15:35.470 --> 00:15:38.040
come to each other’s aid in the event of
war.

00:15:38.040 --> 00:15:42.220
Les than four months later, Germany invaded
Poland to ignite the Second World War.

00:15:42.220 --> 00:15:47.029
Mussolini proved erratic in his response to
the prospect of fighting a European war alongside

00:15:47.029 --> 00:15:48.029
the Nazis.

00:15:48.029 --> 00:15:49.029
One day he appeared jubilant, only to seem
to rue the pact that would bring the wrath

00:15:49.029 --> 00:15:50.029
of the major European powers down on his country
the next.

00:15:50.029 --> 00:15:52.290
On the very day that Germany invaded Poland,
Mussolini announced to his cabinet that he

00:15:52.290 --> 00:15:57.570
had decided not to fulfil his Pact of Steel
obligation on the basis that Germany had signed

00:15:57.570 --> 00:16:02.600
a non-aggression pact with the Soviet Union,
an act that violated provisos of the pact.

00:16:02.600 --> 00:16:07.180
The truth was that Il Duce had absolutely
no confidence in his country’s readiness

00:16:07.180 --> 00:16:08.319
for war.

00:16:08.319 --> 00:16:12.350
Despite his announcement to cabinet, Mussolini
continued to put up a pretence of support

00:16:12.350 --> 00:16:13.350
before Hitler.

00:16:13.350 --> 00:16:16.589
However, in private he spoke of joining the
British / French coalition.

00:16:16.589 --> 00:16:20.959
Finally, on June 10th, 1940, with the Nazis
looking assured of an easy victory over the

00:16:20.959 --> 00:16:24.829
French, Mussolini declared war on Britain
and France.

00:16:24.829 --> 00:16:28.389
Italian troops were quickly sent to France
in order that Mussolini could have some share

00:16:28.389 --> 00:16:29.579
in the spoils of victory.

00:16:29.579 --> 00:16:34.189
At the same time, he mobilized forces into
Africa to attack British holdings there.

00:16:34.189 --> 00:16:38.230
Large scale forces were sent into Egypt, while
other divisions focused on Greece.

00:16:38.230 --> 00:16:42.351
At the same time, Mussolini was overseeing
the air force as they joined with the Nazi

00:16:42.351 --> 00:16:44.370
Luftwaffe in the Battle of Britain.

00:16:44.370 --> 00:16:49.470
In every theatre the ill-prepared Italian
forces were driven back or stalemated.

00:16:49.470 --> 00:16:53.980
This enraged Hitler, who was forced to send
Germany troops in to save the Italians.

00:16:53.980 --> 00:16:58.240
By the beginning of 1942, it was apparent
that Italy was fighting a losing war.

00:16:58.240 --> 00:17:01.330
They were badly beaten in Egypt at the Battle
of El Alamain.

00:17:01.330 --> 00:17:05.400
Then the Allied invasion of French North Africa
pushed them further back on their heels.

00:17:05.400 --> 00:17:08.620
The allied invasion of Sicily was the final
straw.

00:17:08.620 --> 00:17:12.360
Italy’s complete defeat was only a matter
of time.

00:17:12.360 --> 00:17:16.769
An Ignoble End

00:17:16.769 --> 00:17:18.350
The country was in a state of emergency.

00:17:18.350 --> 00:17:21.740
There was no fuel so all the factories were
down.

00:17:21.740 --> 00:17:25.020
Food was incredibly scarce with widespread
starvation resulting.

00:17:25.020 --> 00:17:27.650
The people were desperate and furious.

00:17:27.650 --> 00:17:32.620
In this climate, a massive march on Rome took
place – a march against Il Duce.

00:17:32.620 --> 00:17:37.170
Bowing to the will of the people, King Victor
Emmanuel ordered Mussolini to his palace where

00:17:37.170 --> 00:17:39.890
he informed him that he was removing him from
office.

00:17:39.890 --> 00:17:43.110
Guards then came in and arrested Il Duce and
took him into custody.

00:17:43.110 --> 00:17:47.610
The New Italian Prime Minister, Pietro Badoglio,
was intent on braking the alliance with Germany

00:17:47.610 --> 00:17:49.600
and signing an armistice with the Allies.

00:17:49.600 --> 00:17:53.340
However, in order to stall for time, he kept
up the ruse of alliance with Hitler.

00:17:53.340 --> 00:17:57.840
Unsurprisingly, when Hitler learned that Badoglio
had signed an armistice on September 3rd,

00:17:57.840 --> 00:17:59.730
1943, he was furious.

00:17:59.730 --> 00:18:03.100
He ordered a full-sale invasion of his former
ally.

00:18:03.100 --> 00:18:07.690
Nazi forces used Blitzkrieg tactics to storm
and capture Rome, forcing the King and Prime

00:18:07.690 --> 00:18:09.240
Minister flee the city.

00:18:09.240 --> 00:18:13.370
Some elements of the Italian army attempted
to resist but many of them simply threw down

00:18:13.370 --> 00:18:15.000
their arms and surrendered.

00:18:15.000 --> 00:18:18.640
Meanwhile, Mussolini was being held at a resort-turned
prison at Campo Imperatore in the Alps.

00:18:18.640 --> 00:18:23.029
He knew the situation was chaotic but was
surprised to see German paratroopers landing

00:18:23.029 --> 00:18:24.650
right outside his cell window.

00:18:24.650 --> 00:18:28.700
The Germans quickly overpowered the guards
and made their way into Mussolini’s cell

00:18:28.700 --> 00:18:29.700
where one of them said . . .

00:18:29.700 --> 00:18:33.070
‘Il Duce, the Fuhrer has sent me to set
you free.’

00:18:33.070 --> 00:18:35.890
Mussolini was taken to Hitlers’ East Prussian
headquarters.

00:18:35.890 --> 00:18:40.190
Hitler genuinely admired Mussolini and considered
him a great influence on his own career.

00:18:40.190 --> 00:18:42.730
But he was disappointed to see the man before
him.

00:18:42.730 --> 00:18:46.870
All of Mussolini’s will to continue the
fascist struggle had deserted him.

00:18:46.870 --> 00:18:51.730
The Fuhrer was dumbfounded when Il Duce asked
if he could be permitted to retire from public

00:18:51.730 --> 00:18:52.730
life.

00:18:52.730 --> 00:18:53.730
Hitler would have none of it.

00:18:53.730 --> 00:18:57.820
He installed Mussolini as the puppet ruler
of northern Italy, which was now in Nazi hands.

00:18:57.820 --> 00:19:02.720
For the next eighteen months, Mussolini played
this role, an impotent leader who ruled over

00:19:02.720 --> 00:19:08.120
a people who hated him and that was surrounded
by armed Nazis and approaching Allies.

00:19:08.120 --> 00:19:11.600
With the writing well and truly on the wall,
Mussolini tried to escape to Switzerland on

00:19:11.600 --> 00:19:13.179
April 27th, 1945.

00:19:13.179 --> 00:19:17.870
Just short of the border, he was intercepted
by Communists who took him and his companions

00:19:17.870 --> 00:19:18.950
into custody.

00:19:18.950 --> 00:19:23.529
The following day they decided to execute
him, along with his long-time mistress, Clara

00:19:23.529 --> 00:19:24.529
Petacci.

00:19:24.529 --> 00:19:28.100
After riddling the bodies with bullets, the
Communist drove them into Milan and dumped

00:19:28.100 --> 00:19:30.030
them in the middle of the town square.

00:19:30.030 --> 00:19:34.610
For hours the citizens of Milan took out their
enmity on the man who had led them to disaster.

00:19:34.610 --> 00:19:38.940
His body was spat upon, stoned, beaten and,
finally, strung up by the feet.

00:19:38.940 --> 00:19:43.350
There the body of the once revered leader
was subjected to the abuse of the crowd.

00:19:43.350 --> 00:20:15.409
His corpse was eventually thrown into an unmarked
grave.

